[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" "question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068549 :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" :question/id #uuid "1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" :question/math-academy-id "q-69780" :question/problem "Select the function that is continuous at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, the only one where we can draw the graph of the function without taking our pencil off of the paper in a neighborhood of $x=-1$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca4279200e98b57570030ea6ecce9c04412645ff16d532f302cb13c6bf59c15e.png)\n\nAll other given options have breaks or asymptotes at $x=-1$, so it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda" "answer-a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5" "answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" "answer-65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5" "answer-468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda" :answer/id #uuid "e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/2a/2a384399032456cc37d3769746bf5cbeb12dfacac0bcc9c76d214a4d8424b9f2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5" :answer/id #uuid "a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/32/323242b8d2226dc4ce6b47bf038162135324ddab549213650b37bb57136fa689.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" :answer/id #uuid "372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/3e/3e9b88b3538904a74157f632e7c89a3c1bb1f86264c8193a0d987525f36c47bf.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5" :answer/id #uuid "65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4e/4e2a50b060d3f3042341269ccd2b323156292d52a65b347ed31c9a3c0746c047.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39" :answer/id #uuid "468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/74/749b6f597aad109a21602f7d9fb4bb0708d9b19b4d80e8234d69f0a5df14419e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/id #uuid "dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104016" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c49ba7f220352a8933159a854d6e311baaaf2b6ec1e1f6fbdb78988653a157f.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. $x=-3$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-3,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is also continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"II and III only\".\n\n![](images/f9/f9c5342a872b4fd0ad2783b00d4d170b05844dc057a462832bf0eed41f50ccf4.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827" "answer-9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35" "answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" "answer-c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654" "answer-b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827" :answer/id #uuid "62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35" :answer/id #uuid "9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer/id #uuid "7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654" :answer/id #uuid "c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3" :answer/id #uuid "b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/id #uuid "29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48089" :question/problem "![](images/17/17932f6186d98bb68ff231c07ae1e2b42dbe7c391ff442db6d4e8c9da92a9a82.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=2$, meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  $x=2$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a" "answer-ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec" "answer-f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e" "answer-c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1" "answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a" :answer/id #uuid "4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec" :answer/id #uuid "ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e" :answer/id #uuid "f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1" :answer/id #uuid "c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer/id #uuid "a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069958 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\n \n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069961 :tutorial/content "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\n \n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068989 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186068549 :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$"] ["question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" :question/id #uuid "1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de"] ["question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" :question/math-academy-id "q-69780"] ["question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" :question/problem "Select the function that is continuous at $x=-1.$"] ["question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, the only one where we can draw the graph of the function without taking our pencil off of the paper in a neighborhood of $x=-1$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca4279200e98b57570030ea6ecce9c04412645ff16d532f302cb13c6bf59c15e.png)\n\nAll other given options have breaks or asymptotes at $x=-1$, so it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."] ["question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93"] ["answer-e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda" :answer/id #uuid "e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda"] ["answer-e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda" :answer/value "images/2a/2a384399032456cc37d3769746bf5cbeb12dfacac0bcc9c76d214a4d8424b9f2.png"] ["answer-a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5" :answer/id #uuid "a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5"] ["answer-a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5" :answer/value "images/32/323242b8d2226dc4ce6b47bf038162135324ddab549213650b37bb57136fa689.png"] ["answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" :answer/id #uuid "372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710"] ["answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" :answer/value "images/3e/3e9b88b3538904a74157f632e7c89a3c1bb1f86264c8193a0d987525f36c47bf.png"] ["answer-65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5" :answer/id #uuid "65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5"] ["answer-65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5" :answer/value "images/4e/4e2a50b060d3f3042341269ccd2b323156292d52a65b347ed31c9a3c0746c047.png"] ["answer-468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39" :answer/id #uuid "468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39"] ["answer-468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39" :answer/value "images/74/749b6f597aad109a21602f7d9fb4bb0708d9b19b4d80e8234d69f0a5df14419e.png"] ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/correct "answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710"] ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/choices "answer-e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda"] ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/choices "answer-a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5"] ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/choices "answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710"] ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/choices "answer-65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5"] ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/choices "answer-468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39"] ["question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93"] [17592186068695 :knowledge-point/questions "question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de"] ["question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/id #uuid "dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac"] ["question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104016"] ["question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c49ba7f220352a8933159a854d6e311baaaf2b6ec1e1f6fbdb78988653a157f.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. $x=-3$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$"] ["question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-3,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is also continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"II and III only\".\n\n![](images/f9/f9c5342a872b4fd0ad2783b00d4d170b05844dc057a462832bf0eed41f50ccf4.png)"] ["question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e"] ["answer-62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827" :answer/id #uuid "62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827"] ["answer-62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35" :answer/id #uuid "9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35"] ["answer-9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer/id #uuid "7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d"] ["answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654" :answer/id #uuid "c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654"] ["answer-c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3" :answer/id #uuid "b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3"] ["answer-b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/correct "answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d"] ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/choices "answer-62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827"] ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/choices "answer-9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35"] ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/choices "answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d"] ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/choices "answer-c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654"] ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/choices "answer-b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3"] ["question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e"] [17592186068695 :knowledge-point/questions "question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac"] ["question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/id #uuid "29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e"] ["question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48089"] ["question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/problem "![](images/17/17932f6186d98bb68ff231c07ae1e2b42dbe7c391ff442db6d4e8c9da92a9a82.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?"] ["question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=2$, meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  $x=2$ only."] ["question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190"] ["answer-4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a" :answer/id #uuid "4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a"] ["answer-4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a" :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$"] ["answer-ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec" :answer/id #uuid "ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec"] ["answer-ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec" :answer/value "$x=-2$ only"] ["answer-f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e" :answer/id #uuid "f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e"] ["answer-f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e" :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$"] ["answer-c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1" :answer/id #uuid "c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1"] ["answer-c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1" :answer/value "$x=0$ only"] ["answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer/id #uuid "a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5"] ["answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer/value "$x=2$ only"] ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/correct "answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5"] ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/choices "answer-4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a"] ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/choices "answer-ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec"] ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/choices "answer-f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e"] ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/choices "answer-c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1"] ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/choices "answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5"] ["question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190"] [17592186068696 :knowledge-point/questions "question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e"] [17592186069958 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\n \n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$"] [17592186069961 :tutorial/content "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\n \n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$"] [17592186068989 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186068549 :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\n- $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$"] [17592186069958 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$"] [17592186069961 :tutorial/content "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$"]]}]